WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.580
Peace be upon you, and welcome to the final video of the second module in our fellowship.

2
00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.280
Our session today isn't merely another lecture to add to our notes and call it a day.

3
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:14.480
This is a comprehensive intellectual upgrade to our mindset and way of thinking as health professionals and leaders.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:23.060
Any ordinary person looking at disease spreading in a neighborhood might say: 'Good grief, bad luck, or some airborne bacteria messing with people.'

5
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.080
Yet we are here to completely transform that perspective.

6
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.500
We want to understand how a public health researcher and leader perceives the world through different lenses,

7
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.700
deconstructing complex phenomena with scientific, professional tools that unveil what lies beneath the surface.

8
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:44.240
To achieve this mental upgrade, our roadmap today begins with the mindset of the public health researcher.

9
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.200
Public health does not rely on a single, narrow lens;

10
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.680
it is grounded in a robust framework of theoretical models.

11
00:00:51.680 --> 00:01:02.340
It is like assembling a grand puzzle: a lens examining the body, a lens probing psychology, a lens observing society, and a lens studying environment, policy, and law.

12
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.960
These models are not mere ink on paper left in drawers; they are the compass answering three existential questions:

13
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:15.600
Why does disease emerge in this specific neighborhood while another is spared? Why is there inequality in people's resilience against climate impacts?

14
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.220
And how do we design radical interventions that address the root cause of the problem,

15
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.580
rather than merely applying bandages and temporary pain relievers?

16
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.060
The models from individual to society:

17
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.800
The first revolutionary breakthrough in modern medical thought

18
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.640
was breaking away from the outdated view that treated the human being

19
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.500
like a broken washing machine or engine,

20
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.600
to be fixed with a pill and some brake fluid.

21
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.340
Then came the Biopsychosocial Model,

22
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.680
affirming that health is not merely chemical reactions,

23
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.000
but an ongoing interplay across three dimensions:

24
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.460
The biological dimension: genetics, thermoregulation, blood pressure, and immunity;

25
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:55.620
the psychological dimension: showcasing stress, nervous anxiety, and eco-anxiety;

26
00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:02.400
and finally the social dimension: income, education, housing quality, and healthcare access.

27
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.120
Let us apply this to climate realities:

28
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.360
During a severe heatwave, biologically the body loses fluids and blood pressure drops;

29
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.840
psychologically, irritability kicks in, sleep deprivation mounts, and stress escalates;

30
00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.240
socially, domestic tensions and friction intensify at home,

31
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.420
and the elderly become isolated in un-airconditioned houses.

32
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.300
Here, the health educator does not just say 'drink water';

33
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:28.300
rather, they recognize that psychosocial support is integral to bodily survival.

34
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.220
When analyzing human behaviors and actions,

35
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.080
we possess another ingenious tool:

36
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.260
The Socio-Ecological Model (SEM).

37
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.680
This framework shatters the myth that human behavior stems purely from isolated free will.

38
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.780
Our decisions are enveloped within five nested circles, like the layers of an onion:

39
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.800
starting with the individual and their knowledge,

40
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.200
expanding to interpersonal relationships,

41
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.300
then organizational structures,

42
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.920
broadening to community and cultural norms,

43
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.120
and culminating in the outer ring:

44
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.500
public policies and governance.

45
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.240
Imagine protecting construction workers from heat exhaustion:

46
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.340
At the individual level: the worker knows they must hydrate and seek shade.

47
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:12.780
At the relational level: if his peers on site ridicule him saying, 'What a pampered guy, can't handle the heat?',

48
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.640
he will endanger his life just to avoid appearing weak.

49
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:21.900
At the institutional level: has the contractor provided shaded canopies and drinking water on site, or not?

50
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.100
At the community level: does the neighborhood even provide public water points for passersby?

51
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:34.320
And at the policy level: have the municipality and labor ministry issued mandatory bans on peak-hour work with penalties for violators?

52
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.800
If you only educate the worker in isolation, it is like telling them to swim against a tsunami.

53
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.540
Real change touches all five tiers concurrently.

54
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:47.180
Now we move to our third milestone: determinants and levels of prevention.

55
00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.820
The Social Determinants of Health (SDOH) model reminds us of the striking truth:

56
00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:57.760
human health is shaped far more by daily living conditions than by operating theaters.

57
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.000
The inequitable distribution of resources is the factory of health disparities.

58
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.220
In climate crises, those with financial stability dwell in thermally insulated homes,

59
00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.760
can afford the air conditioning bill, and drink filtered water in peace;

60
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:18.440
meanwhile, someone living under corrugated zinc roofs in an informal settlement cannot afford a fan, and their water comes from tankers vulnerable to runoff contamination during the first rains.

61
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.460
The health educator knows that their health crusade is inseparable from social justice.

62
00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.660
Here comes the prevention pyramid: when do we intervene?

63
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.200
As we discussed in earlier videos:

64
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.440
Primary prevention: we eradicate the hazard at its roots before it approaches the body;

65
00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:33.720
we afforest neighborhoods,

66
00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.100
paint rooftops with reflective white paint,

67
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.220
and deploy early warning systems 3 days ahead of heatwaves.

68
00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.960
Secondary prevention: here we race against time to detect hazard at its inception;

69
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.780
screening workers for dizziness and headache symptoms,

70
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.300
cooling them down before they collapse into heatstroke comas,

71
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.080
and assessing children's respiratory function the moment dust storms strike.

72
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.800
Tertiary prevention: when disaster strikes,

73
00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.640
we intervene inside hospitals to prevent mortality and alleviate complications of renal failure and cardiac arrest alongside rehabilitation.

74
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.340
Our greatest arena as educators is the primary and secondary levels:

75
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.800
building ramparts of safety in the community before individuals reach intensive care.

76
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.080
In our fourth stop, we zoom out to the maximum scale,

77
00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.560
encompassing the entire planet:

78
00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.480
The One Health framework.

79
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.440
This model proves that human, animal, and environmental health

80
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.420
form a single umbilical cord and an indivisible fabric.

81
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.200
Over 60% of hazardous infectious diseases

82
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.860
are zoonotic in origin,

83
00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.580
spilling over to us through deforestation,

84
00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.700
unplanned urban expansion, and wildlife trafficking.

85
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.500
When climate shifts alter temperatures and precipitation patterns,

86
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.080
mosquitoes, ticks, and sandflies expand their habitats,

87
00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.360
transmitting dengue, malaria, and leishmaniasis

88
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.720
into entirely new regions.

89
00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.360
We cannot protect a child from dengue fever

90
00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.480
while open sewage drainage and breeding swamps

91
00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.240
saturate the neighborhood and degrade the environment.

92
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.960
Human health begins with planetary preservation.

93
00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:52.980
In our fifth milestone,

94
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.860
we zoom in on the engineering of change:

95
00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:57.940
health behavior change models,

96
00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.900
such as the Health Belief Model (HBM).

97
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.180
Never fall into the trap of believing

98
00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.040
that knowledge alone suffices to induce change.

99
00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:04.980
No matter how often we reiterate this across our fellowship,

100
00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.680
you must always keep it in mind:

101
00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.780
every smoker knows smoking causes cancer,

102
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.500
and every victim of heatstroke knows the sun is scorching.

103
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:15.500
Behavior change demands perceived threat:

104
00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.860
feeling that the hazard impacts them personally and harms their children;

105
00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:22.560
self-efficacy: believing in one's capacity to adopt the behavior,

106
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.340
knowing it is neither complicated nor costly;

107
00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:25.340
breaking barriers:

108
00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.760
pointing them toward affordable home-based remedies,

109
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.460
instead of prescribing filters costing fortunes.

110
00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.180
And now we reach the pinnacle:

111
00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.760
Integrative Diagnosis:

112
00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.000
assembling the puzzle pieces before the construction worker heatstroke crisis.

113
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.040
Here we look through the Biopsychosocial lens,

114
00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.300
to observe dehydration physiology coupled with the worker's fear of losing daily wages and impoverishment.

115
00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.100
We expand through SEM,

116
00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:51.140
to observe peer pressure on site, contractor greed, and absence of municipal oversight.

117
00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.900
We apply SDOH,

118
00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:57.640
recognizing he only risked his life to put food on the table for his children.

119
00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.520
We then invoke the Prevention Pyramid,

120
00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.840
rejecting reliance merely on tertiary ambulance responses,

121
00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.220
and demanding mandatory shaded shelters and breaks, which constitute primary prevention.

122
00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:12.060
We connect through One Health, observing how lack of urban tree canopy and excess asphalt

123
00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.300
spawned urban heat islands suffocating the work site.

124
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:19.080
And we deploy Behavioral Models to train workers to recognize dizziness symptoms

125
00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.800
and drink water without hesitation.

126
00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.160
This is how a public health leader thinks with a 360-degree vision.

127
00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.600
In summary: public health is not merely disjointed advice;

128
00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.480
it is a comprehensive thought architecture.

129
00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.260
We do not treat superficial symptoms;

130
00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.680
we deconstruct the interplay of body and mind,

131
00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:35.440
society,

132
00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:36.740
equitable resources,

133
00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.460
and climate.

134
00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:38.860
With this rigorous foundation,

135
00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.100
we officially draw the curtain

136
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.420
on the entirety of Module Two.

137
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.580
You now possess the solid intellectual bedrock

138
00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.180
to serve as authentic leaders.

139
00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.840
We leave you with this practical exercise:

140
00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.780
Consider the surge in childhood asthma exacerbations

141
00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.280
during sandstorms

142
00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.700
and heatwaves in your local neighborhood:

143
00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:59.820
How do you apply the SEM across family, school, and policy levels?

144
00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:04.720
What intervention constitutes primary prevention, and what constitutes secondary prevention?

145
00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.620
And how do you utilize the One Health principle to interpret the rising frequency of sandstorms

146
00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.700
in relation to overgrazing and vegetation degradation?

147
00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.180
Thank you for watching,

148
00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.580
and heartfelt congratulations on completing Module Two!

149
00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:18.460
We will see you in Module Three with utmost determination and passion!
